.TH LION-LOCKER 8 "2026" "LionOS" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
lion-locker \- LionOS secure screen lock
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lion-locker
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-\-mock\fR]
.br
.B lion-locker
\fB\-\-check-config\fR | \fB\-\-print-schema\fR | \fB\-\-version\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lion-locker
locks the screen through the
.I ext-session-lock-v1
Wayland protocol. Once the compositor confirms the lock, nothing but the
lock surfaces is visible or focusable, and the compositor keeps the
screen locked even if
.B lion-locker
dies. The session owner is re-authenticated with PAM. The lock screen UI
(\fBlion-lockscreen\fR) is supervised by the daemon and talks to it over a
private Unix socket.
.PP
The daemon is fail-closed: a crash, a UI failure, SIGTERM, a compositor
.I finished
event or a logind
.I Unlock
signal never unlocks the session. The only ways to unlock are a
successful PAM authentication, a PAM-verified single-use grant consumed by
.BR Unlock() ,
or the opt-in grace window.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Configuration file (default /etc/lion/locker.json, or $LION_LOCKER_CONFIG).
.TP
.B \-\-check-config
Validate the configuration and exit (non-zero on error).
.TP
.B \-\-print-schema
Print the JSON Schema of the configuration.
.TP
.B \-\-mock
Demo mode: scripted compositor, PAM accepts the password "lion". The UI
socket and the D-Bus service are real.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Keys live under the top-level object "locker". Spec keys:
.B lock_on_suspend
(default true),
.B grace_period_ms
(0),
.B hide_notification_content
(true),
.B show_media_controls
(true). Documented extensions include
.BR lock_on_lid_close ,
.BR honor_logind_lock ,
.BR emergency_info ,
.BR allow_shutdown ,
.BR greeter_session_id ,
.BR explicit_unlock ,
.BR pam ,
.BR throttle ,
.BR ui ,
.BR rate_limit
and
.BR bus .
Unknown keys are rejected. See
.B --print-schema
for the full list with ranges.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the configuration without dropping the UI or the lock.
.TP
.B SIGTERM
Stop. The screen stays locked (compositor semantics) and the lock marker
is kept, so a restarted daemon re-locks before reporting ready.
.SH FILES
.TP
/etc/lion/locker.json
Configuration.
.TP
/etc/pam.d/lion-locker
PAM service used for re-authentication. If it is missing the daemon
refuses to lock (an un-unlockable lock is worse than none).
.TP
$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/lion-locker/
(or
.IR /run/user/<uid>/lion-locker ,
logind-managed, when XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is unset)
0700 runtime directory: the lock marker
.RB ( locked )
and the UI socket
.RB ( ui.sock ,
mode 0600). The daemon refuses to use a directory that is not owned by
the session user or is group/other-accessible.
.SH D-BUS
Service
.B os.lionos.Locker1
on the session bus: methods Lock, Unlock; properties IsLocked, State,
Failures; signals Locked, Unlocked, AuthFailed. Callers other than the
session owner are denied. See docs/DBUS.md.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Clean stop.
.TP
1
Fatal startup error (no lock protocol, no libpam, bad config, bus name
unavailable) or the compositor connection was lost. systemd restarts the
daemon, which re-locks if the marker exists.
.TP
2
Bad command line.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lionctl-locker (1),
.BR pam (8),
.BR logind.conf (5)
